The Phnom Penh Municipal Court today resumed the first of a series of trials involving 44 opposition party activists charged with plotting and incitement to cause social chaos in connection to the failed attempt by leading opposition figure Sam Rainsy to return to Cambodia from exile on November 9, 2019. The trials had been delayed for several months due to COVID-19.
The trials will be held over seven days on the following Tuesdays, with the next trial set for December 14. The final trial will be held on February 8.
Deputy prosecutor Seng Hieng questioned the pair over what he described as Rainsy’s attempts to overthrow the government and alleged insults against the king.
He also questioned Bunthorn on why the activist had travelled to Malaysia in August 2019, pressing him on who had supported the trip and who he had met there.
29-year-old Phuong Tha, the former president of the CNRP youth movement in South Korea, maintained that he had not been involved with the opposition party since its dissolution.
He also refused the accusation from the prosecutor.
Am Sam Ath, deputy director of human rights group Licadho, said that the ongoing trials were receiving close attention from the international community.
It’s the law enforcement of double standards, as we see.” He told CamboJA that he believed that political negotiation was a better path forward than the court system.
Cambodian-American human rights activist Seng Theary dresses an apsara dancer as she arrives for the hearing at the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, December 7, 2021. CamboJA/ Panha Chhorpoan
“It’s really beautiful to dress in a Cambodian costume like an apsara, but this time and today as a defendant should not dress like this,” he said. “ should dress like the others.”
“Cambodians who wear foreign costumes can join in the court hearing freely, but I dress in Khmer traditional clothes – why do they ban me from participating?
She said that the judge will sentence her on December 28, as there will be less public attention over the holidays.
Speaking at the inauguration of an administration building of the Defense Ministry on December 29, Hun Sen said he planned to make a law limiting the age of prime ministers to 72.
“Before I said I will continue to be a prime minister for another 10 years, then I said I will do it until I wish not to, which means that I may leave within one years, two years… or in another 20 years, it’s up to me, so should understand the meaning clearly.”
Last week, the central committee of the Cambodian People’s Party endorsed Manet as their only candidate to succeed Hun Sen, with Manet expected to be listed on the 2028 ballet.
Political analyst Meas Nee said the CPP needs an appropriate amount of time until the party sees itself fully safe from threats to its popularity.
However, Nee said Manet will depend on his father’s influence to gain control. “But for me I am not surprised whether he could lead the country or not, what I am most concerned about is how he can lead Cambodia back to the democracy path and respect for human rights, which is important.”
Political analyst Lao Mong Hay said Manet’s succession to his father’s position is now wholly assured but whether he will succeed him in 2023 or in 2028 will remain subject to further horse trading within the CPP.
Mong Hay said so far there is no indication that Manet has achieved much to win him public praise or appreciation.
However, he said as a compromise candidate to the premiership, it is very difficult for Manet to assert his power.
Sophal said Hun Sen also wants to ensure the family continues to rule Cambodia in the future. However, he said that 2023 is a bit too soon for Manet to become prime minister, unless Hun Sen faces headwinds that prevent his continued rule.
CPP spokesman Sok Eysan saidthat Manet will become prime minister when the situation is favorable, the exact time of which is not yet clear.
Eysan denied that Manet’s unanimous central committee endorsement was due to his father’s influence, saying that it was based solely on his personal characteristics.
“We know that the old leaders of the CPP were armed struggling and using a lot of violence, so it is difficult for us to compete, sometimes they use ways to crack down on ,” he said.
Koul Panha, an advisor to local election monitor COMFREL, who has been living in following the dissolution of the CNRP and subsequent crackdown on civil society, said that based on Cambodian laws, Manet must resign from his military position when he becomes a political party officer and becomes a PM candidate and political elected representative.
