If this is what you need, I suggest taking a look at Git manual.
It is important to speed up the development, but can generate huge headache (prejudice and rework) If there is a control.
It's been a long time I wanted to write about it, but never found a schedule available, so far.
This model focuses exclusively on Git as a tool for versioning of all our source code.
There is a tremendous “war” around that.
Git no doubt changed the way developers think about doing a Merge or create a branch.
With Git these actions are extremely simple and represent one of the major parts of our work routine, believe me.
As a result of your simplicity and repetitive nature, branching and merging are no longer something to be afraid.
No more talk, Let's go to the development model.
Each developer makes pulls and travel for the origin.
For example, This can be useful for working with two or more developers in a new great functionality, previously sending work in progress for the origin.
In the background, This model of development is quite inspired by existing models around.
We consider origin/master as the main branch where the source code of HEAD always reflects a State production-ready .
Some would call that “branch of integration”.
So, each time changes are incorporated back to the master, It's generated a new version , by definition.
Next to the branches main, master and develop, our development model uses a variety of branches to help support the simultaneous development between team members, What 1) facilitates the tracking of new features , 2) prepare for delivery of a new version and 3) helps to quickly fix flaws in production .
The different types of branches We can use, are:
Each of these branches has a specific purpose and is linked to strict rules, so that, branches can give rise to branch and that branches must be merged the your targets.
Under a technical perspective, These branches are not considered “Special”.
To start the development of a feature, the target version in which this feature will be embedded may well be unknown at this point.
The flag –No-ff causes the merge always create a new object of commit, Although the merge could be performed with a fast-forward .
Reverse a feature one piece (IE, a group of commits), It's a real headache in the last situation, While that is easily done if the flag –No-ff tiver sido usada.
– Should merge-if again the:
The key moment to create a new release branch branching of develop is when the develop is already (almost) reflecting the desired state of new release .
Until that time, the develop branch reflected changes to the “next release” , but it is unclear if this “next version” will eventually be 0.3 or 1.0, until the release branch is started.
For example, Let's say that the version 1.1.5 is the current production release and we have a great release coming.
So, We've expanded and give release branch a name reflecting the new version number:
Here, bump-version.sh is a shell script that changes some files from the working copy to reflect the new version.
This new branch There may be there for a while, until the release be thrown definitely.
The addition of new and large features Here is strictly prohibited.
First, the release branch is merged into master (Since each commit in master is a new version by definition, Remember).
To keep the changes made in the release branch, We need to put them back to the develop.
This step can lead to a merge conflict (probably go, Once we changed the version number).
Now, that really broke up, the release branch can be removed, Since we don't need it anymore:
They arise from the need to act immediately after an unwanted State of a production version .
Changes in the develop leave the still unstable.
The only exception to the rule here is that, When there is a release branch in progress, the changes of hotfix need to be merged to this release branch, instead of develop.
(If the work on the develop immediately requires this bugfix and can't wait until the release branch is completed, you can surely merge the bugfix for deveolp, New Also.)
Although there is nothing really extraordinary in this branching model, the figure at the beginning of the Post can be very useful in our projects.
