USB device not recognized – Printer – Taylor Lee Blog ツ
Scene of the crime: Two HP Multifunction printers with the same problem: “USB device not recognized“.
At first I thought be a problem with the USB printer cable.
After, I left for (Re)install the driver, but when you connect the USB cable from the printer, Behold again appeared the message “USB device not recognized“, with that USB icon next to the windows clock (Systray).
Actually this Post is to say what everyone is reluctant to believe, but believe me, all I did was change the printers USB cable, but it wasn't by any cable!
What confused me to get this solution in the first place was that I had already tried to change the USB cable by another brand new purchased in store.
Well, so here is the most important tip: Don't trust a cable (USB or any other) even though it is new!
Although there between a question: The USB cable was with a physical problem or was just incompatible with the printer?
The USB espeficicação 2.0 has full backwards compatibility with previous versions of the specification (USB 1.0 and 1.1) and incorporates the maximum data transfer rate of 480 MB/s.
All USB-compatible computer systems 2.0 (laptops, notebooks and desktops) must support the three transfer rates: 1,5 MB/s, 12 MB/s and 480 MB/s, peripherals are a little less free.
Peripherals that support the maximum throughput of 480 MB/s are called USB 2.0 High Speed and can use the High Speed logo shown below.
Currently, the Digital Image HP Scanjet scanners offers several USB-compatible High Speed, including the scanners HP Scanjet 4500 c, 4600c, 5500c and 8200c.
Figure 2: USB Symbol
It is important to note that these devices are fully compatible with USB specification 2.0 for high-speed peripherals (12 MB/s), What is essentially the same as the USB specifications 1.0/1.1.
Finally, the USB peripherals that support the minimum rate 1,5 MB/s, as keyboards, mice, joysticks are, sometimes, called Low-Speed USB devices.
In summary, the problem of “USB device not recognized” It is probably caused by low-speed USB cable (Full-Speed).
Remember that this also applies to scanner or other devices, as external HD.
But if you don't want to get rid of your low-speed USB cable it is possible to use a “Fireworks”.
ATTENTION: Do not use this option if your keyboard or mouse is USB, in this case, buy the cable “USB High Speed” or try disable the setup USB support 2.0
You managed to make a walkthrough that discusses various possibilities possible.
Congratulations for the post!
It took me to find the correct cable and I have heard many “stories” which USB cable 2.0 high speed is the same… Right!!!
