Home/National News/ Only Stringent Traffic Law Enforcement Can Survive More Lives on Roads
These three stories are just a few among many others involving motorbike drivers and passengers, including children, most of whom do not wear helmets. According to a report of Cambodia’s General Commissariat of National Police (GCNP), there were 113 road crashes in just the 5 days of the Khmer New Year Festival (13-17 April 2016).
According to a report by AIP Foundation, focused specifically on the passenger helmet law, if law enforcement were stringent from 2014 and helmet wearing rates reached 80%, the lives of at least 561 motorcycle passengers — both adults and children – would have been saved and up to 10,574 people would have been protected from serious head injury, by the year 2020. In terms of economic impact, Cambodia would have saved US$98,618,422, the finding indicates.
“Experience from other countries demonstrates clearly that strong enforcement is extremely effective in raising helmet use rates to over 90%, which translates to saving road users’ lives.In Cambodia, where motorcyclists account for the majority of those injured or killed, a targeted focus on the helmet law can have tremendous impact,” Kim Pagna continued. Kim Pagna expressed a major concern over the sub-decree on enforcement, namely its exclusion of child passengers.
Article 8 of the Cambodian Road Traffic Law states that “A motorcycle can carry 2 people and 1 child. The motorcycle driver and passenger as well as children aged from 3 (three) years old must wear proper safety helmets.
“We encourage the government to take further action to promote helmet use in Cambodia, especially among their government staff and school students. Each government ministry or institution should issue protocol for staff to require those traveling on motorcycles to wear helmets both as drivers or passengers.
Through a comprehensive effort, thousands of lives can be saved from unnecessary injury or death. Gen. Ty Long, Deputy Director of Traffic Police and Public Order Department of GCNP agrees with Kim Pagna saying that although the number of helmet use is increasing since early this year, the number has not reached the target level.
“I’ve learnt about the traffic law but I see that many people still do not wear helmet and they are not fined by police. So, I don’t wear it either sometimes,” he said.
Phnom Penh’s Traffic Police Chief Chev Hak also recognizes that most of the motor passengers use a helmet at daytime, but not at night, a time at which there is a lag in law enforcement.
Gen. Ty Long also echoes the Minister of Interior that the traffic law enforcement must be implemented firmly in order to change the public attitude toward driving on the road and to survive people from accident, especially from head injuries.
