Is it prohibited to eat carbohydrates after 18 hours?
Being of great controversy, the consumption of carbohydrates covers a range of possibilities which must be precisely evaluated and, therefore, balanced in order to decide which are the best ways of its use without causing losses.
What you eat now is not what the body will use now
Daily activities: A next factor to consider
IS REMOVING CARBOHYDRATES THE SOLUTION TO WEATHER?
Carbohydrates are the most acceptable energy source for human metabolism and also the most usable. Perhaps this is the reason why such a group of macronutrients is so studied and, at the same time, so talked about among scientific circles or not.
Constituting a large part of the diet of most people, these nutrients follow different views over the years, being time considered essential for life and time considered great villains.
Among these innumerable protocols that are generated, by reliable or unreliable sources, countless truths are linked to innumerable myths and beliefs.
Among other words, words such as "the consumption of carbohydrates must be cut after 18 pm" or even, "one should consume only carbohydrates with a low glycemic index" start to appear and create paradigms in a large part of the population. But is all of this true?
After all, can we believe these theories, or are they just popular sayings?
Carbohydrates as an energy source, but not in their sole function
Faced with so many functions, it is clear to realize that today carbohydrates are, although partially dispensable in the diet, nutrients which must have a moderately adequate, but existing, frequency of consumption.
More than that, they also come to be understood in view of these many existing studies that there is a moment or a few moments which should or should not be consumed carbohydrates.
More than that, what you eat now, will go through processes of digestion, absorption and metabolism to then be made available for use (or storage). And that takes time.
As fast as carbohydrates are metabolized, they will not have significant impacts in post-consumption moments, unless, of course, we are talking about hormonal impacts, such as insulin production, for example.
It is expensive to realize that, right away, then, consuming carbohydrates at night does not mean such a nightmare, after all, they will not be used / stored at that moment, properly said.
However, the second factor will only happen in significant caloric surpluses, which are, in reality, the major responsible for the gain of body fat.
Could this be true or, following this principle, could we put our trainings in play, as well as their intensity?
This way, normally, your biggest carbohydrate intake should be in this period. Then, he will be able to consume a half amount in the morning, between the first three meals, increase consumption in the afternoon until the periods after training and decrease with the arrival of the night.
Well, in this case, it would normally be recommended a higher consumption of glycids at night and decreased in the morning, refuting almost completely the theory that takes the title of the article.
Seeing the above examples, then, it is simple to see how much "generalized laws" can interfere negatively in the muscle building process, or even in body reduction (remember that there is a need for intense training even in periods of cutting).
A very neglected next factor is the daily activities performed by an individual, which give off differentiated energy expenditure depending on the practice, as well as the intensity of the same.
We cannot consider that every activity requires large amounts of carbohydrates. For example, a typist, who spends hours sitting in front of a computer, does not have high energy shedding throughout the day.
During the performance of these numerous activities, the necessary amounts of carbohydrates that must meet the individual needs of each one must also be carefully prescribed.
Even for individuals who exercise their trades (especially those who give off high energy levels) at night, or even at dawn, they need an adequate intake of carbohydrates and, perhaps higher than during the day, where they rest from anaerobic exercises.
After all, then should we consume carbohydrates only during training periods, regardless of the time of day?
It is a fact that there are many studies today that show that a sudden reduction in the levels of carbohydrates in the diet, as well as their own withdrawal, do not cause performance problems.
Without a shadow of a doubt, we can ingest smaller amounts of carbohydrates, either in the diet as a whole, or in periods of less energy shedding, but even so this must occur properly, impacting the effects of carbohydrates already mentioned.
- Carbohydrates should be used mainly in times of greatest energy release, either with training or daily activities;
- Despite the fact that most individuals have less energy expenditure at night, as they are entering rest periods, individuals who tend to work at night and / or train at night have a higher expenditure during that time, refuting the story that one should consume less carbohydrates in that period;
- The fad diets show very generalized concepts, therefore, it is more than necessary to consult professionals duly capable of proposing an individual plan to the needs of each individual;
