Is it forbidden to eat carbohydrates after 18 pm?
Consuming carbohydrates at night has been one of the biggest discussions among the main magazines of diet, fashion and talk.
Certainly, this is a subject that arouses curiosity in men and women, especially those who seek good shape and follow proper diet and exercise patterns for this.
Is it possible to gain fat by consuming carbohydrates at night or is it possible to maintain a good metabolism with this consumption?
Do the lies about the oppression of the powerful GH have a scientific purpose?
Carbohydrates, indispensable for some functions, are the main protagonists of diets from the most common to the most exotic, in most cases and, therefore, attentively responsible for what they call results (good or bad).
However, it seems that the new nutritional lines have shown that, in fact, what will most influence the good or more results regarding the consumption of this group of macronutrients is the form and quantities of its administration, thus demonstrating a great need for knowledge. and protocols which can establish a better parameter for its use.
In the short term, it can be said that the fallacies about carbohydrates advocate its success and, especially, the dubious looks at its real effects on metabolism.
Should the consumption of carbohydrates obey different rules than the consumption of other macronutrients, showing certain degrees of peculiarity?
Today, we will talk about one of the greatest sayings of modern nutrition: The consumption of carbohydrates after 18pm or, simply, the consumption of carbohydrates at night.
Obviously when talking about carbohydrates, the first function that comes to mind is their energy requirements. Being the main source of energy for the human body, this group of macronutrients not only performs this function, but also performs numerous functions that can vary from hormonal stimuli to the body's water balance.
The great influence of carbohydrates on metabolism is something that draws attention as they are able to stimulate structures which are not necessarily dependent on carbohydrates, properly speaking. There is, for example, the production of serotonin in the small intestine.
It is clearly possible to obtain results, as well as to maintain the body without the continuous intake of carbohydrates, but, normally, it is not possible to obtain a high performance without them, although academically some studies contradict it, claiming it is possible.
These moments of consumption are usually associated with those of greater energy expenditure, such as before and after training in any sport. But does that make sense?
A big mistake is to imagine that what you eat at a given moment is what your body uses at the same moment. Basically, it is as if we believe that the energy used in weight training is due to meal pre-workout done 1,5h before it.
The synthesis of muscle glycogen (main energy source in training with high intensity weights) takes at least 24h-48h. This means that, basically, we are training today with what we ate yesterday (including showing the importance of consistency in the diet).
Logically, we have a slightly higher energy availability in the post-prandial moments, but not in full use of what was ingested.
Carbohydrate intake must be present so that the body can achieve optimal metabolism during the recovery period and, of course, in the preparatory periods for the next training session as well.
What is the best option? So we have that old story of “making the first meal like a king and the last like a beggar.”
To better understand the factors of this carbohydrate consumption, we are going to propose that an individual perform their anaerobic training in the afternoon.
Okay, in this case, we consider a reduction or even a removal of the nutrient from the diet in the afternoon / night, which coincides with the theory already referred to as a myth.
The same would happen with an individual who trains in the early hours of the day: Amazingly, due to prolonged fasting, this individual must have a higher consumption of glycids not only in the early hours of the day, but, in the last meal, choosing, including foods that are more difficult to digest, such as whole-grain pasta or sweet potatoes. So, later on, the carbohydrate consumption of the same afternoon should then be reduced.
Knowing how, how much and where an individual works, studies or performs his duties is more than fundamental to develop an energy consumption principle.
On the other hand, a worker, a carpenter or even a packer, releases a much higher energy level, which is necessary to be taken into account.
In this way, not only training is what influences a whole carbohydrate balance, but the individual's day-to-day life.
The body, despite having its biological clocks and identifying different luminosities, does not know how to tell time, therefore, what will define if an individual will tend to gain weight, gain muscle mass, maintain weight or reduce its fat percentage corporea are the total energy levels ingested as well as the distribution of macronutrients.
It is not because, normally, most individuals spend less energy over the course of the day that this expenditure does not occur.
In fact, it would be practical to think that way. However, as mentioned, not everything we eat will be used in the next few moments.
And this, regardless of the time, that is, it must be done at all times, varying only the amounts and types of carbohydrates to be used.
However, in professional sports practice it is not what has been well observed, placing these researches in a certain degree of doubt in the applicability to high performance.
However, we can learn from the previous information that:
– The body, despite having biological clocks governed mainly by luminosity, does not know how to tell time, so the total energy intake is what will define whether you will gain or lose weight;
- At any time, whether in the morning, afternoon or night, the body will store body fat if there is a large caloric surplus;
- Attention should be paid to the consumption of carbohydrates for muscle recovery and also for the next training session.
Remember: Individual needs are essential to define any dietary points. Take the general recommendations only as a reference and starting point for your own individualization.
Is it worth taking the carbohydrates out of your diet to lose weight for good?
