Meet 10 Super Tips to Improve Digestion - Bodybuilding Tips
Having a fully functioning digestive system is essential for us to process food well and absorb its nutrients (both micronutrients and macronutrients).
So, look for ways to optimize digestion will be of extreme benefit to your health and will directly reflect on your results in bodybuilding practice. In addition, good digestion prevents problems such as: excess of gases, intestinal colds, diarrhea, abdominal pain, gastric stuffing, among other several problems.
1- Do not get clogged with liquids at meals
Not even the fact that you have something to eat with meals today receives much fanfare. That's because, we know that drinking fluids with meals will not cause really significant harm.
Therefore, drinking fluids during a meal will not inhibit your digestion or anything like that, but it can make it a little slower and difficult to occur. Depending on the amount of liquids and food ingested, some particles may not be digested well, impairing the absorption of their nutrients.
Fish are great sources of protein and rich in Omega 3, are excellent not only for their nutritional profile, but also for easier digestion.
This is because they are less fibrous than meats, making the enzymatic contact can be relatively greater. In addition, you still eat very important minerals for the body, such as iodine and phosphorus.
Especially in times when you need to facilitate your digestion, such as pre-training that is very close to the time of training, or even in post-training periods, fish are excellent options.
In addition, dietary fiber improves the glycemic response, thus promoting a better feeling of satiety, which can even be advantageous for those who need lose weight, as you will only eat what you need.
Vegetables also have some anti-nutritional factors, that is, some components present in different types of vegetables, such as phytates e oxalates can impair the absorption of specific nutrients or a range of them.
In this way, full intestinal health is essential. In this way, you will be guaranteeing not only this good absorption, but the flow of the mass which needs to be eliminated from the body in the feces.
If you have problems related to intestinal constipation, frequent diarrhea, nutrient malabsorption, excessive irritations in the intestine, frequent pain or any other types of problems, your digestion will certainly be impaired.
The first one is pay attention to the consumption of ideal amounts of dietary fiber (30-35g / day), and both excesses and deficits are harmful. In fact, the consumption of insoluble fibers is essential for the nutrition of colonocytes (cells that are part of the intestinal mucosa) and for the prevention of colon cancer.
The third way to promote good health to our gut is to ingest lipids which also help in this aspect of transit of the fecal bolus.
). Taking good care of your gut, you will certainly have optimized nutrient absorption!
Many people use antacids or that famous “Fruit salt”, especially when they eat too much and end up “feeling sick on a full stomach”.
Therefore, when we ingest antacids and alkalizing compounds, we cause this pH to rise, making it less acidic. And this is terrible, as it impairs digestion and still leaves the gastrointestinal tract very favorable to having undesirable microorganisms developing.
Note: I'm not telling you to never use antacids, okay?
However, people who have less gastric capacity and / or a diet with many foods, should use shakes to replace their meals completely or partially.
In addition, they do not cause as much gastrointestinal swelling and the enzymatic contact with them is relatively greater, making it easier for you to empty the gastrointestinal, which is good for you to make your meal more smoothly.
For example, assuming you eat 6 solid meals daily (and the post-workout shake), your diet could be divided like this:
Obviously, if you don't have the financial means for so many shakes, for example, you can use it once or twice a day. Thus, it will certainly bring good relief to your gastrointestinal system.
However, processed or packaged foods may contain gluten in their composition, usually due to what we call cross contamination. That is why foods like oats and some corn flours, despite having no gluten, are declared with the presence of this protein.
Gluten has some allergy-causing compounds and its excessive consumption can cause gastrointestinal problems and discomfort and a long time to be digested.
However, if you are one of those who consume food with gluten 3 or 4 times a day, for example, eat bread in the morning, noodles for lunch, bread for afternoon snacks and etc., you should have a little more attention, since you may be more vulnerable to the problems mentioned earlier.
Carbonated drinks should not be consumed more than once a week, and, if possible, they should be further away from meals so that the stomach does not dilate further.
Some infusions (teas) have the potential to help both in digestion processes and in reducing discomfort in the digestive system. These infusions have no energy value and can be consumed frequently without any problem.
Many people go all day without eating, due to the rush or laziness of having a good organization with their food and, at the end of the day, they overeat.
Periods shorter than 2h may also cause discomfort and impair digestion and periods longer than 4h will fall into the same problem mentioned above.
Thus, optimizing digestion in an intelligent way is essential for you to have good health and good results in both bodybuilding and your quality of life.
In this first meal, we seek a high energy density, to break the time we fast when we sleep. For this, we use oats, which are a source of complex carbohydrates, dietary fibers and unsaturated lipids with bananas, to provide carbohydrates that are easier for the body to assimilate.
This mixture, in addition to carbohydrates, also provides proteins of high biological value. Even in the case of proteins of plant origin, when combined, they complement each other in their aminograms, which makes them of high biological value to the body.
For our proteins, none other than red meat!
From proteins, let's go to tilapia. Why not the chicken?
In our pre-workout, which occurs shortly before the start of our workout, a quick shake, containing essential amino acids (including branched chain ones), proteins of high biological value previously digested (and, therefore, the importance of whey being hydrolyzed ) and carbohydrates from waxy maize which, despite easy digestion, do not raise insulin levels in the body.
Don't forget to drink a good amount of water with this shake, as hydration is also essential for good performance and even for your health. About 500-600ml of water at this point is enough.
Again, we have basically the same shake in the post-workout period. What changes, in reality, is the amount of protein, slightly higher due to the greater need for signaling and even supplying calories to the body.
We also know that consuming carbohydrates immediately after training will not, for example, increase your protein synthesis rates. It turns out that, in this case, we make this meal merely to provide the body with calories more quickly (at an opportune time) and to promote some specific signals, thus making it possible to raise the levels of processes which generate better levels of hypertrophy and, for now, hyperplasia.
We also use whole grape juice to provide a significant amount of nutrients Antioxidants to the body, such as resveratrol and vitamin C, as well as a good amount of carbohydrates to go along with those provided in the potato. For the protein source, we have beef again.
For sweet dreams, nothing better than an environment anabolic present in the body. For this, nothing better than proteins of high biological value combined with lipids, since we are going to sleep and we do not need large amounts of carbohydrates.
As for lipids, we focus on the consumption already present in eggs, but, we add a little Brazil nuts, because they are rich in antioxidants and essential nutrients in the protection of the thyroid and in the production of testosterone.
